WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.969
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4523.

2
00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:01.938
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.938 --> 00:00:02.262
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:02.262 --> 00:00:03.877
Today is September twenty-fourth, 2015.

5
00:00:03.877 --> 00:00:09.692
I'm Chelsea Arseneault representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

6
00:00:09.692 --> 00:00:14.862
We're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

7
00:00:14.862 --> 00:00:18.415
Today we're at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

8
00:00:18.415 --> 00:00:21.323
I'm here today with Ms. Paige . . . Ms. Julia Paige.

9
00:00:21.323 --> 00:00:24.231
Thank you so much for agreeing to be interviewed.

10
00:00:24.231 --> 00:00:28.431
This is going to be a short interview, about fifteen to twenty minutes.

11
00:00:28.431 --> 00:00:33.923
We'll likely be in touch with you to set up a longer interview later if you're interested.

12
00:00:33.923 --> 00:00:34.246
<v JULIA PAIGE>Okay.

13
00:00:34.246 --> 00:00:34.569
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

14
00:00:34.569 --> 00:00:38.769
Aright can you please . . . For the record, can you state your full name?

15
00:00:38.769 --> 00:00:39.415
<v PAIGE>Julia L.H.

16
00:00:39.415 --> 00:00:39.738
Paige.

17
00:00:39.738 --> 00:00:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When and where were you born?

18
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.182
<v PAIGE>In DeQuincy, Louisiana.

19
00:00:44.182 --> 00:00:44.909
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

20
00:00:44.909 --> 00:00:52.182
<v PAIGE>[ . . .?] I was born in DeQuincy Louisiana July the fourth, 1929.

21
00:00:52.182 --> 00:00:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And how many siblings did you have?

22
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.500
<v PAIGE>There were six of us.

23
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

24
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
<v PAIGE>There were five brothers and I was the only girl.

25
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.000
They were all older than I. [ . . . ?]

26
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.500
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were number five?

27
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.500
<v PAIGE>I was number five.

28
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And the only girl?

29
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:16.927
<v PAIGE>The only girl.

30
00:01:16.927 --> 00:01:17.236
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

31
00:01:17.236 --> 00:01:21.255
<v PAIGE>So you know I . . . I was . . . I didn't have girls on my street.

32
00:01:21.255 --> 00:01:25.582
I was raised up with my brothers and all the boys to play with.

33
00:01:25.582 --> 00:01:26.200
My brothers.

34
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.218
So I was kind of rough and tough when I was growing up.

35
00:01:30.218 --> 00:01:34.545
I think I was thirteen years old when I finally had a girl friend.

36
00:01:34.545 --> 00:01:36.091
But I did stay around.

37
00:01:36.091 --> 00:01:40.727
At that time I was getting ready to leave myself to go off to school.

38
00:01:40.727 --> 00:01:47.218
You see, DeQuincy was small and one teacher had to teach the two grades at that time. [You want all that?]

39
00:01:47.218 --> 00:01:49.382
<v ARSENEAULT>Here let me clip this on you.

40
00:01:49.382 --> 00:01:50.000
<v PAIGE>And . . .

41
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:50.330
<v ARSENEAULT>Sorry.

42
00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:55.946
I think we picked it up but I'm just going to make sure we get the audio.

43
00:01:55.946 --> 00:01:57.268
<v PAIGE>And . . . I wanted more.

44
00:01:57.268 --> 00:01:57.929
You know?

45
00:01:57.929 --> 00:02:00.902
So Mother allowed me to go off to school.

46
00:02:00.902 --> 00:02:06.518
My first year of schooling in high school I was thirteen, and I went with another lady.

47
00:02:06.518 --> 00:02:08.500
She was going to school there.

48
00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:10.152
She had been there previously.

49
00:02:10.152 --> 00:02:13.125
I went to school one year in Opelousas, Louisiana.

50
00:02:13.125 --> 00:02:15.107
I'm sure you've heard of that.

51
00:02:15.107 --> 00:02:20.723
And then after that I came back home and I went off to a predominantly girl's school.

52
00:02:20.723 --> 00:02:27.000
I went off to a Catholic school, Holy Rosary in Lafayette [Louisiana] and that's where I finished in 1947.

53
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:27.330
So . . .

54
00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:27.661
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

55
00:02:27.661 --> 00:02:32.946
<v PAIGE>When I finished school and it was time for me to go to high . . . to college.

56
00:02:32.946 --> 00:02:35.920
And what I wanted . . . I didn't want to teach.

57
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:38.232
I wanted to be a clothes designer.

58
00:02:38.232 --> 00:02:44.509
But in that day and time she didn't have all the money for me to be a clothes designer.

59
00:02:44.509 --> 00:02:47.812
So I was a little rebellious and I didn't go.

60
00:02:47.812 --> 00:02:50.786
I wouldn't go, because I didn't want to teach.

61
00:02:50.786 --> 00:03:04.000
And it was about . . . You can't believe I'm getting way ahead of the subject if I tell you about how I finished college and when did I get my college . . . So you wouldn't want to know that right now.

62
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:07.690
<v ARSENEAULT>Well I want to know all of it, but I want to . . .

63
00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.842
<v PAIGE>Oh I can tell you about it.

64
00:03:09.842 --> 00:03:14.454
So where I wanted to go . . . See it was tough on Mother in those days.

65
00:03:14.454 --> 00:03:16.299
She reared us without a stepfather.

66
00:03:16.299 --> 00:03:17.837
She didn't believe in stepfathers.

67
00:03:17.837 --> 00:03:19.374
<v ARSENEAULT>What were your parents' names?

68
00:03:19.374 --> 00:03:21.219
<v PAIGE>Will [Hollis?] and Lou Anna [Hollis?].

69
00:03:21.219 --> 00:03:23.064
He got killed on his job.

70
00:03:23.064 --> 00:03:28.598
He worked for the plant in DeQuincy [Louisiana] where they had a . . . They'd hall those logs of wood,

71
00:03:28.598 --> 00:03:30.751
those pine logs, then blast them off.

72
00:03:30.751 --> 00:03:35.670
Then when they blast them off well you know they'd have the saw dust and stuff.

73
00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.745
And one night he fell into one of those bins.

74
00:03:38.745 --> 00:03:40.283
They call it turpentine plant.

75
00:03:40.283 --> 00:03:41.820
She reared us by herself.

76
00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.050
You know kind . . . hard.

77
00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.895
That was during the depression time.

78
00:03:44.895 --> 00:03:47.047
But she did very marvelous with us.

79
00:03:47.047 --> 00:03:49.814
I've heard people say they ate at other houses,

80
00:03:49.814 --> 00:03:56.886
but we weren't allowed to eat at other houses because she provided enough for us as well as send me off to school.

81
00:03:56.886 --> 00:04:03.343
And I've always been . . . I always felt a whole lot of love and proudness for my mother, because she did this.

82
00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:06.111
And she just didn't believe in stepfathers and stuff.

83
00:04:06.111 --> 00:04:08.571
She said nobody was going to eat before

84
00:04:08.571 --> 00:04:08.878
<c.p>us.

85
00:04:08.878 --> 00:04:09.801
And we ate.

86
00:04:09.801 --> 00:04:19.332
She washed and ironed for a living which was too tough for her because she could hardly get the fifty cents or seventy-five cents for a load of clothes, you see.

87
00:04:19.332 --> 00:04:26.712
But then I learned a lot from her because she did wash clothes in the boiling tub here . . . I mean an old boiling tub.

88
00:04:26.712 --> 00:04:28.557
I came up in that time.

89
00:04:28.557 --> 00:04:30.402
But I'm not ashamed of it.

90
00:04:30.402 --> 00:04:34.091
I'm proud of the way I came up, because she was tough.

91
00:04:34.091 --> 00:04:37.781
I learned what I know and how to do now you know.

92
00:04:37.781 --> 00:04:42.701
And that's about . . . My daddy was dead but we lived like two doors from a church.

93
00:04:42.701 --> 00:04:44.546
We had to go to church.

94
00:04:44.546 --> 00:04:46.391
So everything was alright with me.

95
00:04:46.391 --> 00:04:51.925
I didn't even know I was poor, really, because like people say they eat at other people's homes.

96
00:04:51.925 --> 00:04:55.000
No, she cooked for us and we had food.

97
00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.136
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would she cook?

98
00:04:57.136 --> 00:04:58.967
<v PAIGE>Well we'd have like . . . meat balls.

99
00:04:58.967 --> 00:05:02.629
I don't even see meatballs when they're fixed spaghetti now a days.

100
00:05:02.629 --> 00:05:04.765
Meatballs and beans just about every day.

101
00:05:04.765 --> 00:05:08.732
Then we were on the welfare too, and that's another thing I admire.

102
00:05:08.732 --> 00:05:12.088
The welfare was good so as far as I was concerned,

103
00:05:12.088 --> 00:05:16.971
because you got beans and rice and grapefruits and apples and oranges . . . a lot of things,

104
00:05:16.971 --> 00:05:21.853
even clothes at that time . . . blankets, and the boys could get the blue shirts and stuff.

105
00:05:21.853 --> 00:05:23.379
And I learned to sew.

106
00:05:23.379 --> 00:05:25.515
Well, I think that was a gift.

107
00:05:25.515 --> 00:05:28.261
That's why I wanted to be a clothes designer.

108
00:05:28.261 --> 00:05:29.482
I could always sew.

109
00:05:29.482 --> 00:05:34.364
They used to have . . . Like they had the rice in sacks and the beans in sack.

110
00:05:34.364 --> 00:05:38.026
You get one sack this time and have a design on it.

111
00:05:38.026 --> 00:05:41.688
Then you'd have to save that sack until you get another sack,

112
00:05:41.688 --> 00:05:48.096
and then I could put them together to make me a gathered skirt, you see, and put a cancan on it.

113
00:05:48.096 --> 00:05:49.926
You probably wouldn't know about that.

114
00:05:49.926 --> 00:05:53.588
You starch and iron it and you'd be clean and looking good.

115
00:05:53.588 --> 00:05:57.860
I would look as . . . or better than a whole lot of people that had

116
00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:58.165
<c.p>daddies.

117
00:05:58.165 --> 00:05:59.691
You know what I mean?

118
00:05:59.691 --> 00:06:01.827
So I . . . It was alright with me.

119
00:06:01.827 --> 00:06:06.404
I wasn't ashamed of my bringing up or . . . I don’t know how you’d say it.

120
00:06:06.404 --> 00:06:07.625
I really was alright.

121
00:06:07.625 --> 00:06:12.812
I was looking just as good, or better, than some people who had the fathers I thought.

122
00:06:12.812 --> 00:06:13.118
Seriously.

123
00:06:13.118 --> 00:06:18.000
So I suppose that's my . . . my church coming up and my . . . my coming up and . . .

124
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:19.784
<v ARSENAULT>Where did you go to church?

125
00:06:19.784 --> 00:06:20.378
<v PAIGE>Baptist church.

126
00:06:20.378 --> 00:06:22.459
But I finished at the Catholic School.

127
00:06:22.459 --> 00:06:23.649
Can you believe, right?

128
00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:25.135
It was a house there.

129
00:06:25.135 --> 00:06:31.676
This was our house and there's a house and then over there was a church so we had to go to church.

130
00:06:31.676 --> 00:06:34.649
Don’t care what happened, you had to go to church.

131
00:06:34.649 --> 00:06:35.541
Strictly a Baptist.

132
00:06:35.541 --> 00:06:36.730
Strictly one not to.

133
00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:38.811
Okay, that's about the size of that.

134
00:06:38.811 --> 00:06:41.189
Questions you want to ask in between there?

135
00:06:41.189 --> 00:06:42.676
Something I should have said?

136
00:06:42.676 --> 00:06:44.459
<v ARSENEAULT>You said so much good stuff.

137
00:06:44.459 --> 00:06:47.135
I have so many other questions to ask you.

138
00:06:47.135 --> 00:06:48.919
But I'm curious about your grandparents.

139
00:06:48.919 --> 00:06:51.000
What do you know about them?

140
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.049
<v PAIGE>I don't know them as well.

141
00:06:53.049 --> 00:06:55.780
I'm named after my mother's mother, my grandmother.

142
00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:56.122
<v ARSENAULT>Okay.

143
00:06:56.122 --> 00:06:57.829
<v PAIGE>Lee was their last name.

144
00:06:57.829 --> 00:07:00.902
And so you see my name is Julia Lee.

145
00:07:00.902 --> 00:07:03.976
<v ARSENEAULT>Are your related to the Lees that live here?

146
00:07:03.976 --> 00:07:04.317
<v PAIGE>No.

147
00:07:04.317 --> 00:07:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

148
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.643
<v PAIGE>I don't know many of my relatives to tell you the truth.

149
00:07:08.643 --> 00:07:10.464
So . . . And I hated the Lee.

150
00:07:10.464 --> 00:07:16.536
That's why I use my maiden name with my . . . You see me say L. H. because I didn't like Lee.

151
00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:18.054
So I put it together.

152
00:07:18.054 --> 00:07:21.089
People don't ask me about that middle name . . . very seldom.

153
00:07:21.089 --> 00:07:23.214
So that's about the size of that.

154
00:07:23.214 --> 00:07:25.643
<v ARSENEAULT>So your mom never talked about her parents?

155
00:07:25.643 --> 00:07:27.161
<v PAIGE>Yeah and my mother too.

156
00:07:27.161 --> 00:07:30.196
This is one thing I forgot to tell you too.

157
00:07:30.196 --> 00:07:31.411
Mother was a midwife.

158
00:07:31.411 --> 00:07:33.536
She delivered many children there in DeQuincy.

159
00:07:33.536 --> 00:07:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she do it in the home or she would go to the homes of the people?

160
00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:45.245
<v PAIGE>Go to the homes of the people and delivered children . . . She even delivered twins and stuff like that.

161
00:07:45.245 --> 00:07:50.102
Oh just about . . . See I was telling you in that area people were broke.

162
00:07:50.102 --> 00:07:51.837
They didn’t have the money.

163
00:07:51.837 --> 00:07:56.694
But she did . . . I was proud of the fact that she was a midwife.

164
00:07:56.694 --> 00:08:02.245
But then finally she stopped because . . . well they wanted to give a certificate to do it.

165
00:08:02.245 --> 00:08:08.837
But she said the pains the ladies would have she had begun to take those pains too, so she

166
00:08:08.837 --> 00:08:09.184
<c.p>stopped.

167
00:08:09.184 --> 00:08:10.571
<v ARSENEAULT>So she was being . . .

168
00:08:10.571 --> 00:08:12.306
<v PAIGE>She delivered my first child.

169
00:08:12.306 --> 00:08:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Really?

170
00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.097
<v PAIGE>As a matter of fact.

171
00:08:15.097 --> 00:08:15.935
<v ARSENEAULT>How special.

172
00:08:15.935 --> 00:08:20.548
<v PAIGE>Well yeah, it was because money was scarce with me too.

173
00:08:20.548 --> 00:08:22.645
And so my oldest child . . .

174
00:08:22.645 --> 00:08:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How did she . . . What was that like?

175
00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:34.302
<v PAIGE>It hurt. [laughs] That wasn't so bad, considering when you think about . . . I tell you giving birth to a child hurts.

176
00:08:34.302 --> 00:08:41.419
And without medication or anything that's really kind of tough you know what I mean . . . tougher, I suppose.

177
00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:43.000
But she was there.

178
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:46.558
She did it so that's her first grandchild too.

179
00:08:46.558 --> 00:08:48.535
So I guess it's alright.

180
00:08:48.535 --> 00:08:52.093
<v ARSENEAULT>Was . . . What . . . Was it a boy or a girl?

181
00:08:52.093 --> 00:08:53.674
<v PAIGE>It was a boy.

182
00:08:53.674 --> 00:08:55.256
<v ARSENEAULT>What was his name?

183
00:08:55.256 --> 00:08:56.837
<v PAIGE>His name was Johannis.

184
00:08:56.837 --> 00:08:58.814
I don't know anybody . . . J-O-H-A-N-N-I-S.

185
00:08:58.814 --> 00:09:00.000
And he's dead.

186
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.186
My oldest child.

187
00:09:01.186 --> 00:09:11.465
He got killed one night coming from a football game, although five of them got killed at the same time coming down this Houston River Road.

188
00:09:11.465 --> 00:09:15.814
They had gone . . . All my children played some type of sport.

189
00:09:15.814 --> 00:09:18.186
My oldest boy . . . No, that's Johannis.

190
00:09:18.186 --> 00:09:19.767
He played the football.

191
00:09:19.767 --> 00:09:26.093
He died—he was seventeen his last year in high school—coming from a game they had played.

192
00:09:26.093 --> 00:09:33.209
And then my next boy played basketball and he played with The Harlem Globe Trotters for nine years.

193
00:09:33.209 --> 00:09:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

194
00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:37.644
<v PAIGE>And then my other son, my baby son, ran track.

195
00:09:37.644 --> 00:09:44.202
He was very good at that because his son was a . . . He ran and was, what, a quarterback?

196
00:09:44.202 --> 00:09:48.939
Who was that that catches the ball and run with it . . . for Tennessee?

197
00:09:48.939 --> 00:09:54.405
Duke, down in Tennessee . . . So all my children were sort of on my athletic order.

198
00:09:54.405 --> 00:09:55.498
But not me.

199
00:09:55.498 --> 00:09:57.684
So I guess that's my life.

200
00:09:57.684 --> 00:10:00.235
So that's about the size of it.

201
00:10:00.235 --> 00:10:03.150
I still have two of my children living.

202
00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:06.429
Robert Junior lives in . . . He's named after his dad.

203
00:10:06.429 --> 00:10:09.344
He's living . . . He lives in . . . I mean Houston.

204
00:10:09.344 --> 00:10:11.530
And Reggie is in Monroe [Louisiana].

205
00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:12.988
He's a train master.

206
00:10:12.988 --> 00:10:15.538
He didn't want to go to college.

207
00:10:15.538 --> 00:10:21.733
But I made him go and he heard . . . finished because he wanted to work on the railroad.

208
00:10:21.733 --> 00:10:23.555
That's all he ever wanted.

209
00:10:23.555 --> 00:10:27.927
So he finished college at twenty because he didn't . . . The other boy

210
00:10:27.927 --> 00:10:33.757
<c.p>could have gone earlier to the Globe Trotters said, "But you going to finish your schooling."

211
00:10:33.757 --> 00:10:39.587
And so he had to stay there until he finished his school and that was that.

212
00:10:39.587 --> 00:10:44.688
So all my grandchildren have their masters and a degree or a master’s degree.

213
00:10:44.688 --> 00:10:45.417
My grandchildren.

214
00:10:45.417 --> 00:10:48.332
I believe in the education that is needed.

215
00:10:48.332 --> 00:10:50.154
So that's why I stayed.

216
00:10:50.154 --> 00:10:52.704
Went back . . . I went back to school.

217
00:10:52.704 --> 00:10:55.255
I stayed out of school fourteen years.

218
00:10:55.255 --> 00:10:58.170
From high school and then I went back.

219
00:10:58.170 --> 00:10:59.263
Was it fourteen?

220
00:10:59.263 --> 00:11:01.449
Eleven years it was I think.

221
00:11:01.449 --> 00:11:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>When did you move to Mossville?

222
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:06.166
<v PAIGE>I never did move to Mossville.

223
00:11:06.166 --> 00:11:11.941
What happened is when I finished I was old enough . . . or I was pretty old then.

224
00:11:11.941 --> 00:11:15.550
I worked in DeQuincy for . . . two and a half years.

225
00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:25.296
You have a certain amount of students per teacher and it happened at this time that student enrollment and had gone down because the plant had closed.

226
00:11:25.296 --> 00:11:31.071
I tell you where my husband worked and then the railroad had gone down and closed.

227
00:11:31.071 --> 00:11:38.651
So they didn't have the same amount of students for teachers, and I had to move . . . another teacher along with me.

228
00:11:38.651 --> 00:11:45.148
So I had a choice of coming to Mossville or going to Washington Marion in . . . I mean Boston.

229
00:11:45.148 --> 00:11:48.396
Washington, Boston I think it is and Lake Charles.

230
00:11:48.396 --> 00:11:51.645
But I didn't want to cross the bridge driving.

231
00:11:51.645 --> 00:11:56.698
I wasn't the best driver I thought, so coming here was better for me.

232
00:11:56.698 --> 00:12:01.751
So when I say I came here on account of a job, that's why.

233
00:12:01.751 --> 00:12:03.195
And then after then . . .

234
00:12:03.195 --> 00:12:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What year was that?

235
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.018
<v PAIGE>It must have been in 1964, I’m almost sure.

236
00:12:08.018 --> 00:12:10.366
And then when it came to the

237
00:12:10.366 --> 00:12:16.738
<c.p>integration you know they took some teachers first before they had the whole integration you know what I mean.

238
00:12:16.738 --> 00:12:21.433
They chose the first one I think they said or . . . I had no idea.

239
00:12:21.433 --> 00:12:23.780
But anyway I was chosen to go.

240
00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:25.793
I left before the complete integration.

241
00:12:25.793 --> 00:12:28.476
And I was chosen to go to Maplewood.

242
00:12:28.476 --> 00:12:29.146
Predominantly white.

243
00:12:29.146 --> 00:12:31.159
So I worked there three years.

244
00:12:31.159 --> 00:12:33.171
I had my first masters then.

245
00:12:33.171 --> 00:12:38.537
I'd come to work you see that's why I say I didn't do very much here.

246
00:12:38.537 --> 00:12:43.567
I'd get up, get ready, feed my children because I . . . they never ate Corn Flakes.

247
00:12:43.567 --> 00:12:47.256
I cooked biscuits every morning and eggs and bacon and whatever.

248
00:12:47.256 --> 00:12:52.287
And then I'd come and work then I'd go to McNeese [State University] at night.

249
00:12:52.287 --> 00:12:52.957
You know?

250
00:12:52.957 --> 00:12:54.970
About three . . . three nights a week.

251
00:12:54.970 --> 00:13:00.000
Get an hour or two . . . Three hours or something I don't know like that.

252
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.359
Until I had that first masters.

253
00:13:02.359 --> 00:13:08.257
Then after I left from Maplewood after three years, and I went back to DeQuincy,

254
00:13:08.257 --> 00:13:13.369
I worked one year with science and then I . . . Ten years with counseling.

255
00:13:13.369 --> 00:13:21.626
Then I went back in the process of my counseling to McNeese and I got another masters in administration and supervision.

256
00:13:21.626 --> 00:13:24.772
And I worked nine years as assistant principal.

257
00:13:24.772 --> 00:13:26.345
And then I quit.

258
00:13:26.345 --> 00:13:27.524
And I traveled.

259
00:13:27.524 --> 00:13:29.490
Through Arizona, Alaska, China, anywhere.

260
00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.063
I've traveled quite extensively.

261
00:13:31.063 --> 00:13:33.029
And so that was it.

262
00:13:33.029 --> 00:13:37.748
But I'm still here . . . working in my yard, keeping my house up,

263
00:13:37.748 --> 00:13:42.859
going to church, a lot of charity work—Pink Lady [volunteer] in the hospital.

264
00:13:42.859 --> 00:13:45.612
Parks and Recreation Board, president of that.

265
00:13:45.612 --> 00:13:47.971
Work with home health at home.

266
00:13:47.971 --> 00:13:53.869
Well, I do any kind of volunteer work where they don't pay me a penny.

267
00:13:53.869 --> 00:13:55.442
Put it that way.

268
00:13:55.442 --> 00:13:56.621
But that's it.

269
00:13:56.621 --> 00:14:03.699
So I'm proud of that fact, because I'm eighty-six and I still can get up and go around.

270
00:14:03.699 --> 00:14:06.451
I lost a lot of my height.

271
00:14:06.451 --> 00:14:09.597
I was five [foot] eight [inches] . . . five nine.

272
00:14:09.597 --> 00:14:14.709
Arthritis has messed up . . . bent over but I still keep my house clean,

273
00:14:14.709 --> 00:14:20.214
and I work in my yard too so no it's been good to me.

274
00:14:20.214 --> 00:14:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

275
00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:24.140
<v PAIGE>So that ought to be a story there in itself.

276
00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:25.710
So I can go now?

277
00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:27.594
<v ARSENEAULT>[laughs] One last question for you.

278
00:14:27.594 --> 00:14:31.048
I'm curious to know what you earliest memory of Mossville is?

279
00:14:31.048 --> 00:14:33.874
<v PAIGE>When I came here . . . When I first came here.

280
00:14:33.874 --> 00:14:37.329
Well see, I worked in DeQuincy I told you three years.

281
00:14:37.329 --> 00:14:38.585
The principal was strict.

282
00:14:38.585 --> 00:14:40.155
And everybody marched in line.

283
00:14:40.155 --> 00:14:43.295
When I first came to Mossville it wasn't like that.

284
00:14:43.295 --> 00:14:48.947
I must have cried about two or three months trying to get adjusted to Mossville and its standard.

285
00:14:48.947 --> 00:14:54.285
It wasn't what I wanted it to be or as strict or whatever you would say it.

286
00:14:54.285 --> 00:14:56.169
Or discipline . . . Better word to say.

287
00:14:56.169 --> 00:14:58.681
It wasn't disciplined as it was in DeQuincy.

288
00:14:58.681 --> 00:15:00.879
I really didn't want to be here.

289
00:15:00.879 --> 00:15:11.870
I cried for months and finally I got the idea that from the principal they weren't afraid of him and I supposed they was but I thought they should have more respect than they did.

290
00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:17.836
So I just told them one day I would never bring him another discipline problem; I'd handle it myself.

291
00:15:17.836 --> 00:15:19.406
And that's what I did.

292
00:15:19.406 --> 00:15:26.000
Then word got around, I won’t say it so you ain't going to write this down, that I was crazy.

293
00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.446
<v ARSENEAULT>How would you discipline in the classroom?

294
00:15:28.446 --> 00:15:29.495
<v PAIGE>The best way.

295
00:15:29.495 --> 00:15:30.543
So they'd listen.

296
00:15:30.543 --> 00:15:32.989
Well we could spank at that time.

297
00:15:32.989 --> 00:15:33.688
You know?

298
00:15:33.688 --> 00:15:34.038
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

299
00:15:34.038 --> 00:15:36.833
<v PAIGE>So what . . . Then, too, I would buy dinners.

300
00:15:36.833 --> 00:15:41.027
They didn't eat at lunch, shoes, and shirt . . . anything to help them.

301
00:15:41.027 --> 00:15:45.220
People had so many children in they household here to my notion.

302
00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:47.667
So whatever they needed, I helped them.

303
00:15:47.667 --> 00:16:00.597
That's when while I was here and I got my degree in counseling what would happen on campus they would be . . . I'd know it because it was going to come to me and tell me about it.

304
00:16:00.597 --> 00:16:06.188
Then we had . . . When we had to raise money to supply some of the needs here,

305
00:16:06.188 --> 00:16:09.333
Queen drives and stuff my Queen would always win.

306
00:16:09.333 --> 00:16:14.226
I worked with junior high and Ms. Rogers . . . not . . . Ms. Rogers was high school.

307
00:16:14.226 --> 00:16:18.769
There was another lady in the library she's always win in high school.

308
00:16:18.769 --> 00:16:27.855
I wonder what happened if she and I had to compete against each other because she wanted to win and I definitely wanted to win too.

309
00:16:27.855 --> 00:16:31.000
So we raised money through the Queen drive.

310
00:16:31.000 --> 00:16:37.133
Then we'd raise money, too, to have money for football guys to go off when they had to go out of town.

311
00:16:37.133 --> 00:16:38.806
I did a lot of that.

312
00:16:38.806 --> 00:16:45.497
I was on the committee that had to keep the money and stuff like that for school just to . . . it was a poor school,

313
00:16:45.497 --> 00:16:53.861
so we had to do something to try to supply the needs of the children and help them out when they had to go out of town to a game.

314
00:16:53.861 --> 00:16:59.158
Then I'd go home and come back in the evening here to work my games and everything like that.

315
00:16:59.158 --> 00:17:00.830
Then I learned to love Mossville.

316
00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:02.782
I didn't tell you about that part.

317
00:17:02.782 --> 00:17:05.848
When they was to go, I cried didn't want to go.

318
00:17:05.848 --> 00:17:07.800
So I cried coming in, cried leaving.

319
00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:11.703
I tried to get the principal to talk to the supervisor let me stay.

320
00:17:11.703 --> 00:17:14.491
But she said, “No, you going to have to go.”

321
00:17:14.491 --> 00:17:15.606
So I left then.

322
00:17:15.606 --> 00:17:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that in 1969?

323
00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:18.419
<v PAIGE>Must have been ‘69.

324
00:17:18.419 --> 00:17:19.838
‘69 . . . ‘70 . . . ‘71 . . . ‘72.

325
00:17:19.838 --> 00:17:25.158
Yeah, because I stayed there two years and I went back to DeQuincy in seventy-two.

326
00:17:25.158 --> 00:17:27.642
And I done fine over there too.

327
00:17:27.642 --> 00:17:30.125
Everything was alright over there in Maplewood.

328
00:17:30.125 --> 00:17:42.894
I didn't want to leave . . . I mean I wanted to leave because I had traveled at that time eight years down the road as a teacher and I was tired and I wanted to go home.

329
00:17:42.894 --> 00:17:48.925
But I had to sneak away from there because they didn't want me to leave from there.

330
00:17:48.925 --> 00:17:51.053
So then I went back home.

331
00:17:51.053 --> 00:17:59.566
And I started working on my other masters after there and so that's how I got to be the principal . . . assistant principal over there.

332
00:17:59.566 --> 00:18:00.630
So that's it.

333
00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:00.985
<v ARSENEAULT>Great.

334
00:18:00.985 --> 00:18:02.049
One last question.

335
00:18:02.049 --> 00:18:03.823
You kind of answered it.

336
00:18:03.823 --> 00:18:05.951
What does Mossville mean to you?

337
00:18:05.951 --> 00:18:07.015
<v PAIGE>Now or then?

338
00:18:07.015 --> 00:18:09.143
I learned to love Mossville really.

339
00:18:09.143 --> 00:18:11.626
See I cried and all at first.

340
00:18:11.626 --> 00:18:12.691
Really, I did.

341
00:18:12.691 --> 00:18:16.238
And . . . what’s happening now, I hate to see it dissolve.

342
00:18:16.238 --> 00:18:17.302
And I care.

343
00:18:17.302 --> 00:18:22.977
I still care and to not have as many . . . come . . . coming . . . come tell you about it.

344
00:18:22.977 --> 00:18:24.042
Like I care.

345
00:18:24.042 --> 00:18:25.460
I'm standing in DeQuincy.

346
00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:26.525
I hate that.

347
00:18:26.525 --> 00:18:30.426
As a black we don't have much left in life period.

348
00:18:30.426 --> 00:18:38.230
It has been a lot of improvement, but we still need some . . . and this to me is the memory of what was.

349
00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:39.649
And it wasn't bad.

350
00:18:39.649 --> 00:18:41.423
You know what I mean?

351
00:18:41.423 --> 00:18:51.000
I don't suppose it was the best thing in life but it was a way of life for me and my . . . help because of the job.

352
00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.639
But I think it should mean more to others.

353
00:18:53.639 --> 00:18:55.691
It still means a lot to me.

354
00:18:55.691 --> 00:18:57.450
I have forgotten names and stuff.

355
00:18:57.450 --> 00:18:59.209
I don't remember names and stuff.

356
00:18:59.209 --> 00:19:01.262
But I still remember it helped me.

357
00:19:01.262 --> 00:19:04.487
It was during my growing period as a teacher you know.

358
00:19:04.487 --> 00:19:12.110
I had to learn a whole lot while I was here because I had just been in three years so I . . . It means a whole lot.

359
00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:13.576
That's all I can say.

360
00:19:13.576 --> 00:19:16.801
Specifically what, I don't suppose I know it just really does.

361
00:19:16.801 --> 00:19:17.681
It still does.

362
00:19:17.681 --> 00:19:19.440
I hate to see it going,

363
00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:23.838
<c.p>but I know time changes you know and it's supposed to be for the best.

364
00:19:23.838 --> 00:19:27.649
So I can go along with that . . . for some acting crazy about that.

365
00:19:27.649 --> 00:19:34.099
I just wish more was involved in . . . But I can tell you what I know and that's . . . That's the size of it.

366
00:19:34.099 --> 00:19:34.979
That's too much.

367
00:19:34.979 --> 00:19:37.618
They always say I talk too much anyhow so . . .

368
00:19:37.618 --> 00:19:38.497
<v ARSENEAULT>No, that's great.

369
00:19:38.497 --> 00:19:40.550
<v PAIGE>I got to keep my thing up.

370
00:19:40.550 --> 00:19:44.361
<v ARSENEAULT>Well I think that’s a good point to kind of wrap up at.

371
00:19:44.361 --> 00:19:47.000
I think we're at twenty minutes so just . . .

372
00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:47.200
<v PAIGE>Really?

373
00:19:47.195 --> 00:19:48.368
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . on behalf of the center and . . .

374
00:19:48.368 --> 00:19:48.759
<v PAIGE>We through?

375
00:19:48.759 --> 00:19:51.299
<v ARSENEAULT>Well we want to schedule a follow up with you for next week.

376
00:19:51.299 --> 00:19:51.885
Is that okay?

377
00:19:51.885 --> 00:19:53.253
<v PAIGE>Now what is the follow up about?

378
00:19:53.253 --> 00:20:00.287
<v ARSENEAULT>That's the longer one where I ask you all about growing up and I'm going to ask you about . . . because you told me about your mom being in the Depression like a person through the Depression,

379
00:20:00.287 --> 00:20:01.851
so I'm going to ask you a bit.

380
00:20:01.851 --> 00:20:02.241
<v PAIGE>Oh yeah.

381
00:20:02.241 --> 00:20:03.218
Yeah tell me about that.

382
00:20:03.218 --> 00:20:04.000
That's true too.

383
00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:10.089
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah I want to hear more about that, and I'll ask you more about what it was like teaching in Mossville.

384
00:20:10.089 --> 00:20:12.699
It usually lasts about an hour if that's okay.

385
00:20:12.699 --> 00:20:13.279
<v PAIGE>Oh really?

386
00:20:13.279 --> 00:20:13.569
Yeah.

387
00:20:13.569 --> 00:20:13.859
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

388
00:20:13.859 --> 00:20:14.149
<v PAIGE>Yeah.

389
00:20:14.149 --> 00:20:15.309
But like I say,

390
00:20:15.309 --> 00:20:25.167
it was during that time when I didn't even know I was poor you know a lot of people worry about they didn't have anything to eat and people you know on the welfare,

391
00:20:25.167 --> 00:20:27.487
I felt that wasn't no disgrace to me.

392
00:20:27.487 --> 00:20:34.156
Not at all because people have different ideas and if you're eating . . . And we had beans quite frequently, but I still love them.

393
00:20:34.156 --> 00:20:38.216
I haven't gotten tired of beans yet I . . . Some people don't even eat them,

394
00:20:38.216 --> 00:20:43.145
but I eat beans every day . . . different kinds—pinto today or red beans tomorrow or black eyed peas.

395
00:20:43.145 --> 00:20:44.015
<v ARSENEAULT>Some black beans.

396
00:20:44.015 --> 00:20:46.625
<v PAIGE>But really I'm not ashamed of my coming up.

397
00:20:46.625 --> 00:20:48.654
And . . . And like say some say they

398
00:20:48.654 --> 00:20:52.424
<c.p>had trouble with the white and black I didn't . . . I had no problem.

399
00:20:52.424 --> 00:20:55.903
I started working when I was twelve years old . . . worked at cafes.

400
00:20:55.903 --> 00:20:58.513
In the summer time I was out of school.

401
00:20:58.513 --> 00:20:59.673
Mother believed in cleanliness.

402
00:20:59.673 --> 00:21:01.123
You had to be clean.

403
00:21:01.123 --> 00:21:02.572
You had to be clean.

404
00:21:02.572 --> 00:21:09.532
She said if you had two pair of socks wear one today and wash them and have the other pair for the next day.

405
00:21:09.532 --> 00:21:14.171
But . . . And people still say I'm picky, but I'm not I just came up that way.

406
00:21:14.171 --> 00:21:20.840
You had everything . . . She would still check your ears and your neck to see if you were clean when you were going out.

407
00:21:20.840 --> 00:21:22.000
I'm serious too.

408
00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:24.950
And I still do all of that with my children.

409
00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:31.144
I did . . . Let me tell you my . . . When my son died in . . . and you may not want to hear all this.

410
00:21:31.144 --> 00:21:33.504
When my son died . . . got killed or whatever.

411
00:21:33.504 --> 00:21:34.683
They had the wreck.

412
00:21:34.683 --> 00:21:38.223
I had the fire going and I had his bed turned down.

413
00:21:38.223 --> 00:21:42.942
Back . . . I still do that for my children when they come in there with their wives.

414
00:21:42.942 --> 00:21:47.367
I still have the bed turned back and I still have food cooked for them.

415
00:21:47.367 --> 00:21:50.612
They say I shouldn't do that but it doesn't bother me.

416
00:21:50.612 --> 00:21:54.446
I mean I love them so much that's . . . They're my kids you know?

417
00:21:54.446 --> 00:21:55.921
And you know one thing?

418
00:21:55.921 --> 00:21:58.281
I think my kids love me as well.

419
00:21:58.281 --> 00:22:00.345
They're still very, very nice to me.

420
00:22:00.345 --> 00:22:07.424
But I don’t always tell them if I'm sick and they . . . Of course this week I was kind of sick with a terrible cold.

421
00:22:07.424 --> 00:22:08.899
But I don't worry them.

422
00:22:08.899 --> 00:22:14.504
I feel like they have . . . Now when I can't I feel . . . I say I need you to come running.

423
00:22:14.504 --> 00:22:16.863
But I love them I really love them.

424
00:22:16.863 --> 00:22:18.043
They have no idea.

425
00:22:18.043 --> 00:22:20.993
They know they say so but I love my kids.

426
00:22:20.993 --> 00:22:22.763
They very, very nice to me.

427
00:22:22.763 --> 00:22:26.597
And it's no shock anybody in DeQuincy can tell you how I do.

428
00:22:26.597 --> 00:22:30.432
They say you should let them do now but hey that's my kids.

429
00:22:30.432 --> 00:22:31.906
Do I let . . . they grown.

430
00:22:31.906 --> 00:22:38.986
Tell you what, the oldest one that's now sixty-one and the other one talking about him listening to that . . . I'm reading your lips, son.

431
00:22:38.986 --> 00:22:41.935
<v ARSENAULT>Well thank you so much for taking the time today.

432
00:22:41.935 --> 00:22:42.525
<v PAIGE>Alright then.

433
00:22:42.525 --> 00:22:44.000
Let’s say next Thursday . . .

434
00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:44.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4523.

435
00:22:44.429 --> 00:22:44.857
End Session I.]
